  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThunderstorm/Precip Chances:\n\nForecast for a round of severe thunderstorms this afternoon\nthrough this evening remains on track. WV imagery shows a potent \nshort-wave over southern Manitoba this morning that will move \nsoutheast toward Lake Superior through the day. Accompanying the \nupper wave is a seasonally strong jet streak (300 mb winds near \n100kts). At the surface, winds veering to the southwest this \nmorning will bring a plume of upper 60s to low 70s dew points to \nthe region creating a highly unstable airmass by early to mid \nafternoon. With favorable thermodynamic and kinematic fields have \nincreasing confidence in a higher end severe weather event taking \nplace across the region this afternoon into this evening.\n\nOne potential hindrance to this event realizing its full potential \nis a round of morning showers/storms withing the warm advection \nregime. CAMs continue to suggest a cluster or broken line of \nshowers/storms will develop over MN early this morning ahead along an \ninstability gradient and track into western WI around sunrise. These \nstorms would likely be expiating a downward trend they reach \ncentral/northern WI as they out run the better instability, but \nthe remnant cloud debris may delay/modify building instability. \nCAMs generally have this activity dying out by 10AM this morning. \nThese trends will bear watching as any differential heating \nboundary/outflows may serve as initiating boundaries later into \nthe day. By mid morning attention will shift to an area of rapidly\ndestabilizing air east of the incoming cold from southern IA to \nthe WI/UP border. As the aforementioned upper-short wave starts to\nimpinge upon this unstable air expect storms to start initiating \nacross northwest an  nort-central WI around 3-4PM (could be as \nearly as 1PM). Initially expect a broken line of super cells as \n0-6 shear vectors are angled about 30 degrees off the cold front \nwhich should help to keep cells discrete initially. Think these \ninitial cells will pose the greatest strong tornado (EF2+) and \nlarge hail (2+ inch) threat as the deep shear will promote well \norganized and persistent updrafts, especially any storms that \ndeviate to the right of the mean wind. With time and eastward \nextent forcing along the front and increase storm coverage should \nlead to cold pool aggregation and the development of one or move \nbowing segments that will pose a damaging (70+ mph) wind threat as\nwell as brief spin up tornados. The severe weather threat is \nexpected to persist all the way to the lakeshore with storms \nexiting the region around 8-10PM tonight. \n\nIn addition to the severe threat high rain rates are expected with \nthese storms, however, the scattered nature of the storms and \nprogressive movement likely will limit widespread higher rain \namounts, but localized 2 inch amounts could occur. Urban areas or \npoor drainage areas will be susceptible to higher rain rates and \nlocalized flooding.\n\nBehind the front Tuesday scattered diurnally diver showers may \npop-up across northern WI with northwest winds over Lake \nSuperior. Forecast sounding show this should be very shallow \nconvection so don't expect much if any lightning with these \nshowers. Beyond Tuesday a building ridge should keep the middle of\nthe week dry with a possible return in showers/storms late in the\nweek into next weekend, but predictability is low. \n\nSmoke Trends:\n\nSurface obs in central WI are showing slowing decreasing visibility \nearly this morning with smoke trapped under an inversion. These \ntrends should reverse by mid-morning as the boundary layer starts to \nmix. Increase southwesterly surface winds today should also greatly \nhelp with clearing surface smoke from west to east. HRRR/RRFS do \nshow another possible plumb of smoke pushing into the region Tuesday \nand Wednesday as winds turn northwesterly, however, there is a high \ndegree of uncertainty with how dense smoke may be.\n\nTemp Trends: \n\nToday will be the warmest day of the week with highs in the middle \nto upper 80s. Cooler temperatures arrive behind the cold front and \nlinger through much of the week.",
